<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Fill in a form with their name and age with adult help. Interactive: Write simple phrases with errors on a drawing or postcard.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write their name and simple phrases on a weather-related drawing (e.g., labeling a personal drawing of a sunny day with their name and a simple phrase describing the drawing). Can write simple phrases related to the weather (e.g., "It is sunny.") with support. Can label pictures of different weather conditions (e.g., drawing a cloud and writing "cloudy").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone! What is the weather today?" and uses weather flashcards (sun, rain, clouds, wind) to elicit choral responses like "It is sunny!" or "It is raining!" The teacher plays "What's Missing?" with flashcards, asking students to identify which weather card is removed. (Pronunciation: /s/ in "sunny", /r/ in "raining", /w/ in "weather").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple drawing of a sunny day on the board. "Look! It is sunny." The teacher then writes "It is sunny." clearly below the drawing, emphasizing the letters and sounds as they write. Then, the teacher draws a cloud and writes "cloudy". The teacher models writing their own name below the drawing. "My name is [Teacher's Name]." (Grammar: Subject + verb + adjective).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I drawing or writing now?" (Expected answer: "Writing!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did I write for the sun?" (Expected answer: "Sunny!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large picture cards depicting various weather conditions (e.g., a bright sun, rain falling, fluffy clouds, a blowing wind) with simple, written labels below them, such as "It is sunny.", "It is raining.", "It is cloudy.", "It is windy." (Vocabulary: sun, rain, clouds, wind, sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can describe simple weather conditions (e.g., "It is sunny.") and ask basic questions, such as "What is the weather today?" to learn about daily conditions. The student begins to engage in simple weather-related conversations (Sociolinguistic).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays the weather picture cards with their corresponding phrases.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to each picture and reads the phrase aloud, "It is sunny." "It is raining." "It is cloudy." Students repeat chorally.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then focuses on the written words, tracing them with their finger while students identify familiar letters. (Phonemic awareness: Identification of sounds of letters of the alphabet in familiar vocabulary).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher holds up a picture (e.g., a rainy cloud) and asks, "Which phrase describes this picture?" and shows two written options (e.g., "It is sunny." and "It is raining."). Students point to the correct written phrase.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing short phrases using Present continuous (e.g., "It is raining.") and Subject + verb + adjective (e.g., "It is sunny."). Vocabulary: sun, rain, clouds, wind, sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Tracing Weather Words):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with large, traceable weather words (e.g., "sun", "rain", "wind") and simple phrases (e.g., "It is sunny.", "It is raining."). Students use pencils to trace the letters and words, focusing on correct letter formation. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting grip/strokes.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled practice (Matching and Writing):</b> Students receive a worksheet with small pictures of different weather conditions (e.g., a cloud, a sun, rain) and a word bank (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy). Below each picture, there is a blank line. Students choose the correct word from the bank and write it under the corresponding picture. Example: Picture of a cloud --> "It is ________." (Students write "cloudy").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction/Peer check:</b> Students show their completed tracing and matching worksheets to a partner. They point to their written words and say, "This is 'sunny'." "This is 'rainy'." Partners give a "thumbs up" if correct or gently point to an error with teacher guidance. The teacher reminds them to use a soft voice and be kind.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual creative drawing and writing product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a blank piece of paper and crayons/colored pencils.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 1: Drawing (Connected to Scenario/Theme):</b> Students draw a picture of their favorite weather or the current weather (e.g., a sunny day, a rainy day, a cloudy sky). The teacher encourages them by saying, "Draw your favorite weather! Is it sunny? Is it raining?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 2: Writing Name (Productive Writing):</b> The teacher reminds students to write their name clearly at the top of the paper, modeling on the board again if needed. "Write your name here."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 3: Writing a Simple Phrase (Interactive Writing):</b> Below their drawing, students write a simple phrase describing their weather picture, with teacher support. The teacher writes example phrases on the board for reference: "It is sunny." "It is raining." "It is cloudy." Students choose one and copy it below their drawing. The teacher circulates, offering help with spelling and letter formation as needed, especially for "It is raining." (Grammar: Present continuous, Subject + verb + adjective).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 4: Sharing:</b> Students briefly show their drawing and point to their written phrase to a peer or the teacher, saying, "My picture. It is [sunny/rainy/cloudy]."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the writing activity and review of student products for completion and effort.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their weather drawing with their name and the simple weather phrase written below it. The teacher collects the drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student writes their name clearly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student attempts to write a simple weather phrase (e.g., "It is sunny.") with support.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The written phrase generally matches the drawing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in letter formation and overall presentation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write my name on my paper?" (Students can nod yes/no or show their paper).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write about the weather in my drawing?" (Students point to their phrase).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What weather word did I write today? Can I say it?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to write the words?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to trace words and match them correctly. Writing the full phrase "It is raining." or "It is sunny." still requires significant adult help and scaffolding for many students, especially with spacing and capitalization. The drawing aspect was highly motivating. I need to continue providing clear models and possibly sentence frames for the next writing tasks. For the next lesson, we will integrate these writing skills into a broader activity, possibly creating a simple weather chart or a class book.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing lesson lays the groundwork for Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), where students will consolidate their learning by contributing to a collaborative weather project, potentially involving simple written labels or descriptions of different weather types to be shared with others.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>